#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""fatterpad-abxy: per-game face-button layout on the Thor's SteamOS (FatterPad's
ABXY half, Android twin: thor/gyropad, profiles.json abxy).

The Thor's face buttons are printed Nintendo-style (A right, B bottom, X top,
Y left). InputPlumber's Thor config (capability map ayn_mcu_nintendo) sends
them to Steam by their printed letters.

My rule (same as FatterPad on Android): games get the buttons by
POSITION, like an Xbox pad ("positions", the DEFAULT): when a game says A,
you press the BOTTOM button (printed B); the right one is B. This daemon
loads an InputPlumber device profile built on the stock Default profile
(positions swaps South<->East, letters swaps North<->West; see SWAPS), live
over D-Bus (LoadProfilePath), with no pad reconnect. The Nintendo layout ("letters",
the printed A on the right is A) is the exception, per game: our emulator
launchers (thor-launch, thor-browse) keep it, because their emulator configs
were set up for it (RetroArch: ra_pad.py). Eden Duo maps by position itself,
so it gets positions and the printed A still lands on Switch A. Steam's own UI
is the Switch layout too (the hardware IS a Switch-layout
pad, so in Steam the A on the right is A): "steam_ui": "letters".

Identity: the Thor reaches Steam as the analog handheld ("deck":
analog triggers, a real Quick Access button) everywhere, Steam's own UI
included; ThorLook makes Steam show it as the Thor. A game that ships Nintendo
prompts gets NINTENDO MODE instead: the Switch Pro identity, Steam Input off
for that game (so Proton hands it the Switch Pro itself, with
PROTON_ENABLE_HIDRAW from environment.d) and the Nintendo layout; its ZL/ZR are
on/off, as on a real Switch. Which games: PCGamingWiki's "Nintendo prompts"
answer for the user's own library (prompts-db keeps that list), and a manual
choice per game always wins. Steam Input is switched off ahead of launch for
those games (Steam reads it at launch) and restored when a game leaves the
mode; the identity switches as soon as Steam starts launching the game.

It follows the game gamescope has in front (GAMESCOPE_FOCUSED_APP on :0):
every Steam app id, window class and launcher can have its own layout, and
the choice is remembered. A switch waits until no face button is
held, so a button held across it never sticks.

Config (~/.config/fatterpad/abxy.json, re-read when it changes):
  {"default": "positions",
   "steam_ui": "letters",                    # Steam's own UI; "default" follows default
   "launchers": {"thor-launch": "letters"},  # substring of the launch command
   "classes": {"<wm_class>": "letters"},     # e.g. an emulator
   "apps": {"<steam appid>": {"mode": "letters", "nintendo": true, "name": "..."}}}
Precedence: app, Nintendo mode, class, launcher, default.

Usage:
  fatterpad-abxy run                  the daemon (systemd --user unit)
  fatterpad-abxy status               what is in front and which layout it has
  fatterpad-abxy flip                 switch the game in front and remember it
  fatterpad-abxy default letters|positions         the default for games
  fatterpad-abxy set letters|positions [--class]   same, explicit; --class
                                      saves it for the window class (every
                                      game of that emulator) instead
  fatterpad-abxy forget [--class]     drop the saved choice for the game in front
  fatterpad-abxy identity switch-pro|deck [--app N]   what the Thor reaches
                                      Steam as for that game (deck = analog
                                      triggers); with no game, or --default,
                                      the default (deck)
  fatterpad-abxy identity forget [--app N]
  fatterpad-abxy nintendo on|off|forget [--app N]   Nintendo mode for that game
                                      (forget = follow PCGamingWiki again);
                                      takes effect at its next launch
  fatterpad-abxy classify [--app N]   how Steam's data sorts a game (auto rules)
  fatterpad-abxy classify --selftest  the auto rules on known games
  --app <steam appid>                 act on that game instead of the one in
                                      front (the Decky panel passes Steam's
                                      running app); with Steam's UI in front
                                      the default is the last game that was
"""
